N('mohawk-chapel-brantford',"Her Majesty's Royal Chapel of the Mohawks",'Brantford','on','worship','standing',43.12443,-80.235,'exact','open','drive','','1785–',
  "Built in 1785 by the British Crown and given to the Mohawk people under Joseph Brant, in return for their support of the Crown in the American Revolution. King Edward VII made it a Chapel Royal in 1904. Its own page calls it the first Protestant church in Upper Canada and the oldest surviving church in Ontario. It is a National Historic Site, and Joseph Brant's remains were moved into a tomb here in 1850 (Wikipedia).",
  "A small church with eight stained glass windows put in between 1959 and 1962 that show Six Nations history, and the tombs of Joseph Brant and his son John. The chapel is run by Six Nations Tourism and belongs to the Anglican Diocese of Huron. Photographing or filming on the grounds needs approval (its page).",
  'The oldest church in Ontario, built for the Mohawks in 1785.',
  "301 Mohawk Street, Brantford. Run by Six Nations Tourism, part of the Six Nations of the Grand River Elected Council. Its visit page: a large lot beside the chapel with free parking; self-guided tour 10 dollars, guided tour 15 dollars; tours outside the season by appointment; elevator access.",
  [W("Her Majesty's Royal Chapel of the Mohawks"),L('The chapel: visit page','https://mohawkchapel.ca/visit/')],
  hours='Victoria Day weekend to Thanksgiving weekend: Tuesday to Sunday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.; other times by appointment',brick='?'),
N('eldon-house-london','Eldon House','London','on','landmark','standing',42.98484,-81.25545,'exact','open','drive','','1834–',
  "A Georgian house of 1834 on what was then 11 acres, built for Captain John Harris, who named it for the Earl of Eldon. The Harris family gave the house and gardens to the City of London in 1959 and it is now a museum (Wikipedia). Wikipedia says the house keeps the family's heirlooms and souvenirs, among them African weapons brought back by George Henry Ronald Harris from his mining years.",
  "The house itself, shown with Harris family belongings, and its gardens. Wikipedia does not describe which furnishings are original; its own page does not either, so the interior was not checked further.",
  'A Georgian house of 1834 that a family kept full of its belongings until it gave the lot to the city.',
  "481 Ridout Street North, London; owned by the City of London. Its own page: open by donation, 5 to 10 dollars suggested; closed June 23 to 25, 2026 for exterior work. Parking was not checked.",
  [W('Eldon House'),L('Eldon House: visit','https://eldonhouse.ca/')],
  hours='Jan–Apr Thu–Sun; May and Oct–Dec Wed–Sun; June–Sept Tue–Sun; 12 to 5 p.m., last entry 4:45',brick='?'),
N('gibraltar-point-lighthouse','Gibraltar Point Lighthouse','Toronto Islands','on','light','standing',43.61367,-79.38531,'exact','view','walk','','1808–1956',
  "Authorized in 1803 by the Legislative Assembly of Upper Canada and built 1808 to 1809 of stone quarried at Queenston, it was paid for by a harbour fee on boats entering the harbour. It was raised from 52 to 82 feet (25 m) in 1832 with Kingston stone, and its light was put out in 1956. Wikipedia calls it the oldest lighthouse still standing on the Great Lakes. Its first keeper, John Paul Radelmüller, was said to have been killed in 1815 by soldiers from Fort York; later research supports the killing but not the dismemberment of the legend. Two soldiers were tried and acquitted.",
  "A tall tapered stone tower on Hanlan's Point at the west end of the Toronto Islands. It stands unused and is only occasionally open, as during Doors Open Toronto.",
  'Toronto\'s oldest ghost story is attached to this 1809 tower on the islands.',
  "Operated by the City of Toronto; look from outside. The Toronto Islands are reached by the city's ferries from the foot of Bay Street; ferry times and Hanlan's Point landing were not checked.",
  [W('Gibraltar Point Lighthouse')],brick='no'),
N('fort-york-toronto','Fort York','Toronto','on','fort','standing',43.63889,-79.40361,'site','open','drive','forts','1793–',
  "The first garrison was set up here in 1793; the fort was rebuilt in 1813 to 1815 after the Battle of York. Seven buildings date from that rebuilding: the two blockhouses, two enlisted men's barracks, the brick officers' barracks and mess, a brick-walled magazine and a stone-walled magazine. The eighth, the blue officers' barracks, is a reconstruction of 1986. National Historic Site of Canada since 1923 (Wikipedia).",
  "The blockhouses, barracks and magazines inside earth ramparts, now with the city's towers and the Gardiner Expressway beside them. Free 45-minute guided tours run on the hour.",
  'A War of 1812 fort with seven original buildings, hemmed in by a highway and condo towers.',
  "250 Fort York Boulevard, Toronto; owned by the City of Toronto and managed since 2000 by a board of citizen appointees. The City's page: free admission; main lot at the visitor centre under the Gardiner, 1 dollar per half hour; another lot at 800 Fleet Street.",
  [W('Fort York'),L('City of Toronto: Fort York','https://www.toronto.ca/explore-enjoy/history-art-culture/museums/fort-york-national-historic-site/')],
  hours='Wednesday to Sunday 11 a.m. to 4 p.m.; free tours on the hour, last at 3 p.m.',brick='some'),
N('brocks-monument-queenston',"Brock's Monument",'Queenston','on','landmark','standing',43.160139,-79.053056,'exact','public','drive','','1853–1859',
  "A 56 m (about 185 ft) column on Queenston Heights for Major-General Isaac Brock. A first monument was built from 1823 and opened in 1824; an explosive charge damaged it badly in 1840 and it was pulled down. The present one went up from 1853 to 1856 and was opened on October 13, 1859 (Wikipedia). It is part of Queenston Heights National Historic Site, kept by Parks Canada.",
  "A 235-step spiral stair leads up to a small platform under the statue. Parks Canada says visitors can climb it. The material of the column was not checked.",
  'A 56-metre column on the heights where Brock fell in 1812, built twice because the first was blown up.',
  "Queenston Heights, 14184 Niagara River Parkway, Niagara-on-the-Lake; Parks Canada. Its page: the site is accessible all year and visitor services run from May to October; monument hours, fees and parking were not on the page.",
  [W("Brock's Monument"),L('Parks Canada: Queenston Heights','https://parks.canada.ca/lhn-nhs/on/queenston')],
  hours='Grounds open all year; visitor services May to October (monument climb times not checked)',brick='?'),
N('laura-secord-homestead','Laura Secord Homestead','Queenston','on','landmark','standing',43.16395,-79.05523,'exact','open','drive','','1800s',
  "Laura Secord lived in this house in Queenston from 1803 to 1835. In the War of 1812 it was shot at and looted, and Wikipedia says it was restored in the late 1900s. Niagara Parks has owned it since 1971. Its page tells the story of her 32 km walk in 1813 to warn the British of an American attack. The year the house was built is not stated on either page.",
  "A white house with arched windows, shown on a guided tour of about 45 minutes by costumed guides, with period objects.",
  'The home of the woman who walked 32 km to warn the British in 1813.',
  "29 Queenston Street, Queenston; Niagara Parks Commission. Its page: open May to October with timed tickets, 10.50 dollars for adults plus tax; free parking.",
  [W('Laura Secord Homestead'),L('Niagara Parks: Laura Secord Homestead','https://www.niagaraparks.com/visit/heritage/laura-secord-homestead/')],
  hours='May to October, timed 45-minute tours (days and times not checked)',brick='?'),
N('bruce-mines-church-on-the-rock','Bruce Mines: Church on the Rock and the copper mines','Bruce Mines','on','industry','standing',46.30042,-83.78793,'site','open','drive','','1846–1921',
  "Copper drew settlers to Bruce Mines in 1846, and the town's page says it produced the first hard-rock copper in Canada in 1847. The mines closed in 1876 after flooding, cave-ins and falling profits, were worked again from 1915 to 1921 until Mond Nickel stopped them, and were decommissioned in 1944 (Wikipedia). The museum is in the Church on the Rock, a Presbyterian church of 1894 that later served as post office and school.",
  "The museum holds more than 7,000 objects, among them Postmaster Joshua Coatsworth's journals of 1850 and a customs ledger from the company wharf. The town's Simpson Mine Shaft, with a replica horse-turned whim and a walk-through shaft, is shut for repairs with no reopening date given; its pin is not set here.",
  'A copper town whose first mine shaft is now a museum, with an 1894 church.',
  "Bruce Mines Museum, 9227 Highway 17 East; run by the town. Its page: Tuesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. from July 1, other times by appointment (705-206-0759); fees not on the page. The Simpson shaft was closed when the town's page was read: do not enter any open mine opening.",
  [W('Bruce Mines'),L('Town of Bruce Mines: museum','https://brucemines.ca/tourism-recreation/museum/'),L('Town of Bruce Mines: Simpson shaft','https://brucemines.ca/tourism-recreation/simpson-mine-shaft/')],
  hours='Tuesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. in summer (from July 1); other times by appointment',brick='?'),
N('moose-factory-centennial-park','Moose Factory: the old Hudson\'s Bay post','Moose Factory','on','fort','standing',51.25401,-80.60478,'area','open','boat','forts','1673–',
  "The Hudson's Bay Company set up Moose Fort in 1673. The French took it in 1686 and the English burned it in 1696, and nothing of that fort remains. A new fort went up a mile upstream in 1730 and the post grew into the company's main James Bay base after 1821. The company's nineteenth-century buildings were made a National Historic Site in 1957 (Wikipedia).",
  "Standing: the Staff House of 1847 to 1850, the oldest building in the James Bay area and the last HBC officers' house; a blacksmith shop of 1849, used until 1934; the stone powder magazine of 1865 to 1866; and Joseph Turner's house of 1863. Centennial Park's open-air museum opened in 1967 and has a graveyard whose oldest stone is dated 1802. St. Thomas' Anglican Church nearby was built from 1864 to 1885.",
  'The oldest buildings on James Bay, reached by train and boat.',
  "Moose Factory is an island community, reached by the Ontario Northland Polar Bear Express to Moosonee and then by water taxi in summer, the ferry or an ice road in winter (Wikipedia). Hours of the museum were not found.",
  [W('Moose Factory')],hours='?',brick='?'),
N('mill-of-kintail','Mill of Kintail','Mississippi Mills','on','industry','standing',45.2464,-76.2533,'site','open','drive','','1830s–',
  "A grist mill of the 1830s on the Indian River, later the summer home and studio of the doctor and sculptor R. Tait McKenzie, a native of Ramsay Township (Wikipedia). It now sits in a 152-acre conservation area kept by the Mississippi Valley Conservation Authority.",
  "The mill houses a museum of McKenzie's sculptures and memorabilia and a second one on James Naismith, the Ramsay Township man who invented basketball. Trails are open all year.",
  'A 1830s mill that became a sculptor\'s studio, with the inventor of basketball in the next room.',
  "2854 Ramsay Concession 8, Mississippi Mills; Mississippi Valley Conservation Authority. Its page: pay at the entrance machine per vehicle (coins or card, a day pass is good for four hours); museum open in the summer months, hours not given.",
  [W('Mill of Kintail'),L('Mississippi Valley Conservation: Mill of Kintail','https://www.mvc.on.ca/mill-of-kintail/')],
  hours='Museum in summer months (days and times not checked); trails open all year',brick='?'),
N('silver-islet-ontario','Silver Islet','Silver Islet','on','town','standing',48.33084,-88.81971,'site','view','drive','','1868–1884',
  "Silver was found on a tiny islet in Lake Superior in 1868. Sibley's Silver Islet Mining Company built wooden breakwaters round it and filled in crushed rock until the islet was over ten times its first size, with a small mining town on the shore. Shafts went to 384 m, and water flooded them early in 1884; the workings were never pumped out and later tries in 1919 and the 1970s failed (Wikipedia).",
  "The miners' houses still stand, now private summer cottages. Wikipedia says the general store has been restored and serves light meals. Sleeping Giant Provincial Park's visitor centre has an exhibit on the mine.",
  'A silver mine on a rock in Lake Superior that the lake finally drowned.',
  "The houses are private: look from the road. The store is a business; its hours were not checked.",
  [W('Silver Islet')],brick='?'),
N('cobalt-mining-museum','Cobalt Mining District','Cobalt','on','industry','standing',47.39557,-79.6859,'site','view','drive','','1903–',
  "Silver was found near Long Lake, later Cobalt Lake, in 1903 and the town was founded that year and incorporated in 1906. Output peaked in 1911, mining fell off in the 1930s, and the last company, Agnico Eagle, stopped in the mid-1980s. The Cobalt Mining District was made a National Historic Site of Canada in 2002 (Wikipedia).",
  "Wikipedia says the Heritage Silver Trail, a driving tour with signs, leads to many of the remaining headframes and mill sites, and that the Cobalt Mining Museum shows the world's largest display of locally mined silver and starts guided tours of an old underground mine. Underground workings, trenches, pits and shafts can collapse and many are fenced off.",
  'A boom town whose silver headframes still stand along a signed driving trail.',
  "The museum is on Silver Street. Its hours and the tour times were not found; the mine sites have different owners and some are fenced: keep to the signed places.",
  [W('Cobalt, Ontario')],brick='?'),
